# Exception Handling

Never throw raw `Error` objects or plain strings. Always throw an instance of a class that extends `CustomError` from `common/errors/custom-error.ts`.

## Existing error types

| Class | Status | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `BadRequestError` | 400 | The request is semantically invalid |
| `NotAuthorizedError` | 401 | The user is not authenticated or lacks permission |
| `NotFoundError` | 404 | A requested resource does not exist |
| `RequestValidateError` | 400 | Express-validator returns validation failures |

## When to create a new error type

If none of the existing types fit, create a new class in `common/errors/` following this template:

```typescript
import { CustomError } from './custom-error';

export class SomeNewError extends CustomError {
  statusCode = 4XX; // appropriate HTTP status code

  constructor(public message: string) {
    super(message);
    Object.setPrototypeOf(this, SomeNewError.prototype);
  }

  serializeErrors() {
    return [{ message: this.message }];
  }
}
```

Key requirements for every new error class:
- Extend `CustomError`
- Set `statusCode` to the correct HTTP status
- Call `Object.setPrototypeOf(this, YourClass.prototype)` in the constructor
- Implement `serializeErrors()`

## Examples

```typescript
// BAD
throw new Error('Item not found');
throw 'something went wrong';

// GOOD
throw new NotFoundError('Item not found');
throw new BadRequestError('Invalid quantity');
```
